    "q-134167": {
      "dom_id": "step-q134167",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Calculate $\\text{Cov}(x,y)$ for the following data sets:\n\n$$\nx=\\{1,\\,4,\\,5,\\,6\\},\\,y=\\{4,\\,5,\\,2,\\,1\\}\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "The (population) covariance of the data sets $x=\\{{x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}\\}$ and $y=\\{{y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n}\\}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}(x,y)=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}$ and $\\overset{―}{y}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i}$ are the means of the data sets.\n\nFirst, we need to compute the means:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot (1+4+5+6) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 16 \\\\  & =4 \\\\ \\overset{―}{y} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot (4+5+2+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 12 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the covariance of our data sets is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(1-4)(4-3)+(4-4)(5-3)+(5-4)(2-3)+(6-4)(1-3)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(-3)(1)+(0)(2)+(1)(-1)+(2)(-2)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[-3+0-1-4] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot (-8) \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
